Spring Boot REST API Development Notes
1. Create DTO Classes

Create Request and Response DTOs to exchange data between the client and server. DTOs help avoid exposing entity objects directly and provide better control over API contracts.

public class ProductRequest {

    private String id;
    private String name;
    private String description;
    private String category;
    private Double price;

    // Getters and Setters
}

public class ProductResponse {

    private String id;
    private String name;
    private String description;
    private String category;
    private Double price;

    // Getters and Setters
}

2. Create Controller Class

Controller classes handle incoming HTTP requests and return responses to the client.

@RestController
@RequestMapping("/products")
public class ProductController {

}

Important Annotations
@RestController

Marks the class as a REST Controller.
Automatically converts Java objects into JSON responses.
@RequestMapping("/products")

Defines the base URL for all Product APIs.
@GetMapping
@PostMapping
@PutMapping
@PatchMapping
@DeleteMapping

Used to map specific HTTP methods to controller methods.
3. Create Service Layer

Create an interface and implementation class inside the service package.

Service Interface
public interface ProductService {

    ProductResponse createProduct(ProductRequest request);

    ProductResponse getProductById(String id);

    List<ProductResponse> getAllProducts();

    ProductResponse updateProduct(String id, ProductRequest request);

    void deleteProduct(String id);
}

Service Implementation
@Service
public class ProductServiceImpl implements ProductService {

}

Important Annotation
@Service

Marks the class as a Service Layer component.
Contains business logic.
4. Create Repository Layer

Repository layer is used to interact with the database.

@Repository
public interface ProductRepository
        extends JpaRepository<Product, String> {

}

Important Annotation
@Repository

Marks the interface as a DAO/Repository component.
Handles database operations.
5. Dependency Injection

Objects for Controller, Service, Repository, and Configuration classes should be managed by the Spring Container.

Use Dependency Injection instead of creating objects using the new keyword.

Autowiring Service in Controller
@Autowired
private ProductService productService;

Better Approach (Constructor Injection)
@RestController
@RequestMapping("/products")
public class ProductController {

    private final ProductService productService;

    public ProductController(ProductService productService) {
        this.productService = productService;
    }
}

6. Create Entity Class

Entity class represents the database table.

@Entity
@Table(name = "PRODUCT")
public class Product {

    @Id
    private String id;

    private String name;

    private String description;

    private String category;

    private Double price;
}

Important Annotations
@Entity

Marks the class as a JPA Entity.
@Table(name="PRODUCT")

Maps the class to a database table.
@Id

Defines the primary key.
